{
	title: 'brave tool: web search through Brave Search',
	spec: 'A `brave` model tool (src/host/tools/brave.ts) gives every model, Claude and GPT alike, the same web search through the Brave Search API (GET https://api.search.brave.com/res/v1/web/search, X-Subscription-Token header). Input: query (required, non-empty), num (default 5, at most 10, sent as count). A request gives up after 30 s, like read_url. The result is plain text: one "title\\nurl\\ndescription" entry per web result, HTML tags stripped and entities decoded, entries separated by a blank line; "No results found." when empty. The key is the credentials file\'s brave entry (an apiKey, read through apiKeys.get); no environment variable. With no key the tool is still offered and returns an error result naming the credentials file and /intro. A non-2xx answer is an error result with the status and at most 500 chars of the body. The turn abort signal cancels the request. Tests use a fake Brave server: formatting, the num cap, the missing-key error, a non-2xx error. The former Serper (Google results) tool lives on as extras/serper.ts, an opt-in plugin that adds GOOGLE with the serper.apiKey from the credentials file; artifact old-google.ts is the old Hal version (reference).',
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'auth.serperKey() reads serper.apiKey from the same liveFile store as provider accounts (only when the file exists, so a key-less home still works with SERPER_API_KEY). The tool object carries url() and format() so tests point it at a local Bun.serve. Checked live against Serper on 28 Sep.',
		"Switched from Serper to Brave on 5 Nov 2026 (196-lug): Serper credits ran out and Brave's monthly free credit covers about 900 searches a month across two hosts. Brave uses its own index, not Google's. The google name stays with the Serper extra, so old histories keep their GOOGLE cards (tool-details still titles them)."
	],
	needs: ['sj']
}
